.\" osfp(1) \(em written with the man(7) macros only, which every target's man
.\" reads, AIX included. Check with: mandoc -Tlint docs/man/*.1
.TH OSFP 1 2026-09-23 osfp "User Commands"
.SH NAME
osfp \- fingerprint an operating system and report how it drifted
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B osfp
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B osfp baseline
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B osfp compare
.B \-b
.I FILE
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B osfp info
.B \-b
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B osfp verify
.B \-b
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.br
.B osfp version
.RB [ \-\-short ]
.br
.B osfp help
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B osfp
records a fingerprint of a freshly installed operating system \(em the path,
type, mode, owner and SHA\-256 of every file \(em and later reports how a live
system has drifted from it: what was added, deleted, modified in content, or
modified in mode and ownership.
.PP
It is meant for occasional audits, not for monitoring: take a fingerprint at
installation, compare weeks or months later, read the report.
It keeps no state between two runs.
.PP
.B osfp
is a single static binary, built without cgo, for Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD,
NetBSD, Solaris, illumos, macOS and AIX.
.PP
Options may be written with one dash or two:
.B \-root
and
.B \-\-root
are the same option.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B baseline
Scan the filesystem and write a fingerprint.
See
.BR osfp\-baseline (1).
.TP
.B compare
Scan the filesystem again and report how it differs from a fingerprint.
See
.BR osfp\-compare (1).
.TP
.BI "info \-b " FILE
Print what a fingerprint holds: the tool that wrote it and when, its key, the
system and host it describes, whether it was taken as root, its scope, its
entry count and size, its digest, and the exclusions it recorded.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the same metadata as a JSON object.
.TP
.BI "verify \-b " FILE
Recompute the SHA\-256 of the whole fingerprint and check it against the
digest stored in it.
This detects a truncated transfer or a damaged medium; it is not a signature.
With
.BR \-\-quiet ,
print nothing and report the result through the exit status only.
.TP
.B version
Print the version, the commit, the Go toolchain, the target, and the
fingerprint key of the running system with the source it was read from.
With
.BR \-\-short ,
print the version number only.
.TP
.BI "help " \fR[\fIcommand\fR]
List the commands, or the options of one of them.
.PP
.BR info ,
.B verify
and
.B version
read no filesystem and need no privileges.
.B baseline
and
.B compare
require root: a scan run as an ordinary user cannot read large parts of the
filesystem, and would produce a fingerprint that looks complete but is not.
.SH FINGERPRINT KEY
Each fingerprint carries a key naming the system it describes, built as
.IR os \- distribution \- version \- arch ,
for instance
.BR linux\-debian\-12\-amd64 ,
.BR freebsd\-15.0\-amd64 ,
.B solaris\-11.4\-amd64
or
.BR illumos\-omnios\-r151056\-amd64 .
AIX adds its technology level:
.BR aix\-7.3\-tl02\-ppc64 .
.PP
The key names the fingerprint file by default, and
.B compare
refuses to compare a system with a fingerprint whose key differs: an
upgraded system would report most of its files as changed.
.B "osfp version"
prints the key of the running system.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, to any value, the text report is never coloured.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR compare ,
no difference found, or differences found without
.BR \-\-fail\-on\-diff ,
or only expected noise found.
.TP
.B 1
.B compare \-\-fail\-on\-diff
found differences outside the volatile directories.
.TP
.B 2
Runtime error or invalid invocation; a fingerprint that fails
.BR verify .
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient privileges, or a comparison between a fingerprint taken as root
and a scan that is not, or the reverse.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by SIGINT or SIGTERM.
No partial fingerprint is left behind.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record a fingerprint of a freshly installed system; it is written to
.IB key .osfp
in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# osfp baseline
.fi
.RE
.PP
Months later, report the drift, and fail in a script if anything outside the
logs changed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# osfp compare \-b linux\-debian\-12\-amd64.osfp \-\-fail\-on\-diff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a fingerprint after copying it to another machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ osfp verify \-b linux\-debian\-12\-amd64.osfp
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
The modification time is recorded but never compared: a change is a change of
content, mode, owner, type or link target.
.PP
Outside Linux, reading a file updates its access time; on a filesystem mounted
without
.BR noatime ,
a scan changes the access times it reads.
They are not compared either, so this never shows up in a report.
.PP
The
.B aix/ppc64
build has never been run on a real AIX system.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osfp\-baseline (1),
.BR osfp\-compare (1)
